data: {"delta":{"partial_json":"{\"file_path\": \"/home/<USER:1>/.local/share/cs-campaign/output/campaign-report.md\", \"content\": \"# Campaign report: greet, tested\\n\\n## Outcome\\nMission met. All three stated criteria satisfied.\\n\\n## What was delivered\\n- `src/greet.py`: `greet(name)` returns `f\\\"Hello, {name}!\\\"`.\\n- `tests/test_greet.py`: `unittest`-based tests covering a normal name\\n  (`greet(\\\"World\\\") == \\\"Hello, World!\\\"`) and the empty string\\n  (`greet(\\\"\\\") == \\\"Hello, !\\\"`).\\n- `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` exits 0 (2 tests, OK).\\n\\nBoth files were authored and committed by `dev` on\\n`cs-sandbox/<DEV:1>.<GROUP:1>`, commit `7d510e258d861bf505ae348e3e88bd5e7d751445`\\n(\\\"Add greet function with unit tests\\\"). No other files were touched.\\n\\n## Verification performed by orchestrator (not just trusting dev's report)\\n- Fetched dev's branch (`cs-campaign-member fetch dev`); confirmed tree\\n  differs from base with real changes.\\n- Read the actual committed source of both files via `git show` against\\n  `refs/remotes/campaign/dev/subject` and confirmed:\\n  - `greet()` implements the exact required format.\\n  - The test file covers both the normal-name and empty-string cases.\\n- Checked out dev's commit into an isolated worktree (`/tmp/verify-dev`) and\\n  independently ran `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests`, confirming exit\\n  code 0 with 2 tests passing (matching dev's self-reported output).\\n- Accepted dev's dispatch `d002` only after this independent verification.\\n\\n## Process notes\\n- Single dispatch to dev was sufficient; no rework was required.\\n- Orchestrator's own branch (`cs-sandbox/<ORCHESTRATOR:1>.<GROUP:1>`)\\n  carries no code commits, by design: the orchestrator role owns planning,\\n  dispatching, and judging, and does not write to the subject repository\\n  itself. The deliverable lives on dev's accepted branch.\\n\"}","type":"input_json_delta"},"index":0,"type":"content_block_delta"}

